 "verdicts": {
  "A longitudinal case study of a real-world consumer app using agentic infrastructure to personalise marketing messaging over 11 months compared an active phase (marketers curated content, audiences and strategies) with a passive phase (agents operated autonomously from a fixed library of components).": 1,
  "After manual content injection and optimisation stopped for 7 months, autonomous marketing agents sustained a +57% (±0.11) lift in notification clicks, +2.4% (±0.21) days with engagement per user, and +0.2% (±0.27) lift in intent and conversion metrics, not reverting to baseline.": 1,
  "During the active human-managed phase of the marketing personalisation study, days with direct app opens attributable to push notifications rose +65.3% (±1.31), days with positive engagement +2.8% (±0.19), days with intent +0.3% (±0.23), and days with conversions +0.3% (±0.24).": 1,
  "In the marketing personalisation case study, agents assembled messages by selecting atomic content components (e.g. value propositions, greetings, visual assets) via a sequential decision-making framework.": 1,
  "Moving to fully autonomous operation dampened lift by roughly 15% for overall engagement, 12% for notification opens, and 26% for days with intent and days with conversion events per user, relative to the active phase.": 1
 }
}